# One frame through the inference template's two providers, Windows, 2026-11-27.
#
# This is the number references/runtime-comparison.md section 5 had written down
# as a gap: a wall-clock CUDA-EP-against-CPU-EP frame time on this machine. The
# wheel in .venv has no CUDA provider (its list is AzureExecutionProvider and
# CPUExecutionProvider), so the measurement is taken with the bundled package's
# own DLLs, by a program that links the same onnxruntime.lib and loads the same
# onnxruntime.dll / onnxruntime_providers_cuda.dll a bundle ships:
#
#   .validation/ort-gpu-1.32.0/bin/bench-ep-frame.exe <model.onnx> <W> <H> \
#       [--iterations 10] [--warmup 3] [--threads N]
#
# Scratch, like the other probes in .validation: bench-ep-frame.cpp is built by
# .validation/build-bench.bat <ort-root> into that package's bin directory, so
# the DLL search finds the packaged runtime before anything on PATH. It creates
# a CPU session, then a CUDA session with the template's six provider keys -
# device_id=0, use_tf32=0, cudnn_conv_algo_search=HEURISTIC,
# cudnn_conv_use_max_workspace=0, do_copy_in_default_stream=1,
# arena_extend_strategy=kSameAsRequested - and prints the provider's own
# serialisation of them (below, once; the later runs print the same string).
#
# A frame is ModelRunner::Run()'s per-frame path and nothing else: wrap the
# caller's host buffer as an OrtValue (no copy, no allocation), Run() with
# default RunOptions, memcpy the output back into a host buffer. With host
# pointers and do_copy_in_default_stream=1 that Run() synchronises, so a sample
# is the whole round trip a host waits for, not a launch.
#
# Machine: Windows 11 10.0.26300, RTX 3060, CUDA 13.5 UMD, toolkit 13.0.48, the
# same machine every other number in that document came from. Runtime: ONNX
# Runtime 1.32.0, API 32 - the version .github/actions/onnx-runtime pins, and
# the package templates/onnx-inference-ofx bundles.
#
# The 1.20.1 CUDA-12 package in .validation/ort-gpu-1.20.1 was tried and is not
# in this log: the same bench built against it printed the CPU row and then
# crashed while creating the CUDA session (no output after that line, exit 139
# in the shell), so there is no 1.20.1 row to report. That failure is a fact
# about this scratch harness on this machine and nothing in the plug-in is
# inferred from it.
#
# Models: the template's own models/test_conv.onnx at 3x48x64 - 9,216 values,
# the shape its suite runs - and .validation/big_conv.onnx, the graph section
# 5's TF32 numbers were measured on (Conv 3->64 3x3, Relu, Conv 64->64 3x3,
# Relu, dynamic H/W, checked with onnx.checker, seed 1234), at 640x480 and
# 1920x1080.
#
# Reading rules, all of them visible per run: the first frame is printed apart
# from the warm ones because it pays kernel loading; the warm-up frames are not
# in the samples; each run measures two rounds in opposite order, so a clock or
# thermal ramp would show up as a difference between them; every sample is
# printed rather than just the median; and "CUDA against CPU on the same frame"
# is ORT's output minus ours, which is 1e-7-ish with TF32 off and would read
# 0.000e+00 if the CUDA row had silently fallen back to the CPU.
#
# Threads: 1 is what the template asks for (ModelRunner.cpp, SessionThreads, and
# the label prints "1 thread"). The 8-thread run is the CPU provider's best case
# at 1080p, measured because a reader will ask what the CPU could do with the
# cores the host has.


