# What the four runs say, read off the lines above (medians of the two rounds,
# every sample printed above them):
#
#   3x48x64      CPU 0.05 ms      CUDA 0.12 / 0.10 ms   - the GPU is 2.0-2.4x
#                                                     slower on 9,216 values
#   640x480      CPU 460 / 463 ms  CUDA 16.6 / 16.5 ms   - 27.7x / 28.0x faster
#   1920x1080    CPU 2,990 / 3,025 ms   CUDA 111.7 / 112.4 ms - 26.8x / 26.9x
#   1920x1080,   CPU 992 / 988 ms       CUDA 114.4 / 113.9 ms - 8.7x
#   8 threads
#
# Three things follow from the lines rather than from arithmetic:
#
#   - The GPU earns its place at 640x480 and above, and loses at the suite's own
#     shape. At 9,216 values the frame is smaller than the launch and the
#     synchronisation it takes to get an answer back, which is the same fact the
#     Vulkan section states for its 95 ms host round trip: a GPU pass on a tiny
#     frame is a transfer with a kernel inside it.
#   - Threads do not move the GPU row (114.4 with 8 against 111.7 with 1, inside
#     the round-to-round spread) and move the CPU row by 2.6-3.1x, so the 26-27x
#     at one thread is the template's own configuration and the 8.7x is what a
#     CPU that uses the cores can close it to. Neither run makes the CPU
#     competitive at 1080p on this graph.
#   - The first frame is not the frame: 410 ms at 1080p against 112 warm, and
#     137 ms at 3x48x64 against 0.10 - kernel loading, once per process. A host
#     that times its first render measures that, not the steady state.
#
# Not measured here: a second GPU, a second driver, a tiled host, a pinned host
# buffer, a multi-pass graph, and any model outside these two. Each frame above
# is one inference of one graph with a host buffer on both ends.
